# TURN relay for online games. Copy to .env.local and fill in.
#
# Phones on different networks (mobile data, guest Wi-Fi, strict routers) often can't connect
# directly and need a relay. PeerJS's free relay servers no longer exist, so online rooms only
# work across networks with one of these set. Any TURN provider works, e.g. a free account at
# expressturn.com or metered.ca gives you the URLs, username and password.
#
# These end up in the public site code, so use credentials meant for browser clients.
VITE_TURN_URLS=turn:relay.example.com:3478,turn:relay.example.com:3478?transport=tcp
VITE_TURN_USERNAME=
VITE_TURN_CREDENTIAL=

# Sunny Square, the 3D world, talks to its own server (../backend). `npm run dev` starts that
# server by itself. A built site needs it hosted somewhere that runs Node (Firebase Hosting
# only serves files) and its address here; without one the world stays off the menu. Leave it
# unset in development, where the dev server passes the world through itself.
# VITE_WORLD_URL=wss://world.example.com/world-socket
